{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an independent evaluator of downstream actions under changed user state. Use only the hidden state transition, visible source evidence, question, and anonymized responses. Ignore method names and self-labels.\nFor each response evaluate:\n- action_state_aligned: the concrete action/plan follows the new-state consequence.\n- stale_action_rejected: it does not continue the action basis implied by the old state.\n- evidence_consistent: details are supported by evidence or cautious inference.\n- non_generic: the action is specific enough to guide the requested task.\n- safe_unknown: it uses UNKNOWN/qualification when evidence is insufficient.\n- pass: true only if action_state_aligned, stale_action_rejected, evidence_consistent, and non_generic are true.\n- error_type: CORRECT_ACTION, STALE_ACTION, GENERIC_ACTION, UNSUPPORTED_ACTION, INVENTED_DETAIL, RETRIEVAL_MISS_SAFE_UNKNOWN, or AMBIGUOUS_QUALIFIED.\nAll boolean fields must be JSON booleans, never Yes/No strings. Return exactly one JSON object with exactly this outer shape: {\"evaluations\": {\"A\": {...}, \"B\": {...}}}. The evaluations keys must be exactly the supplied A/B codes.\n\n[Hidden reference]\nM_old: I tend to decline most evening invitations because I really prefer spending my free time at home.\nM_new: Lately I feel most recharged when I’m out in the middle of things—checking out new spots, meeting people, and keeping my calendar full with plans.\nHidden logic: Now prefers an outgoing, socially active, plan-filled leisure lifestyle rather than a home-centric, low-social one.\n\n[Visible evidence]\n=== Session 3 [Time: 2022-05-12 19:00] ===\nUser turn 1: Do you think it’s possible to have a genuinely satisfying social life without going out a lot at night, and if so, what are some creative ways people make that work?\nAssistant turn 2: Absolutely, it’s possible. Plenty of people have rich social lives without being out every night. It just takes a bit of intention and choosing ways to connect that actually fit your personality and energy.\n\nSome people make it work by turning everyday things into social time, like cooking dinner with a friend, having a weekly show‑watching night, or doing a hobby together. Others build routines that naturally bring people together, like morning coffee meetups, weekend walks, or joining a class or club that meets during the day.\n\nGroup chats, low‑pressure hangouts at home, or even online communities can fill in the gaps too, especially if you’re the type who prefers deeper one‑on‑one conversations to big nighttime outings.\n\nThe key is regular, meaningful contact. If you have that, it doesn’t really matter whether it happens at midnight in a bar or at 10 a.m. over coffee.\nUser turn 3: That makes sense. If you had to pick a few “default” options that are social but still low-key (ideally daytime or at-home), what would you suggest—and how do you keep them from feeling like you’re always the one hosting or initiating?\nAssistant turn 4: A few things tend to work well as easy, low‑key defaults: inviting someone over for a simple activity like cooking, playing a game, or watching something; meeting for a walk or coffee; or doing a shared errand or hobby together. They’re casual enough that they don’t feel like “events,” but still give you real connection.\n\nTo avoid being the only one hosting or initiating, it helps to set things up in a way that naturally spreads the effort. For example, you can frame it as a shared routine—something like “Want to trade off hosting movie nights?” or “Let’s do Sunday walks; whoever’s free can pick the route.” You can also casually ask others what they feel like doing next time, which nudges them to take the lead without it becoming awkward.\n\nMost people actually like being given an easy structure to plug into. Once you create the rhythm, it usually starts to run itself.\nUser turn 5: I tend to decline most evening invitations because I really prefer spending my free time at home. Given that, what are a few “default” social plans I can suggest that fit that preference, and what are some low-awkward ways to signal I’m happy to participate but don’t want to be the one always hosting or initiating?\nAssistant turn 6: You can lean into things that feel natural at home without making you the default host. A couple of easy options are suggesting a show‑watching night where you rotate living rooms, or inviting someone to join you for a “parallel hang,” like reading, working on projects, or cooking something simple together. Meeting in the daytime for a walk, coffee, or errands also fits your home‑oriented vibe without pulling you into late nights.\n\nTo avoid becoming the constant organizer, you can frame plans as shared routines rather than invitations. Saying something like “I’m trying to do a Sunday afternoon walk most weeks—feel free to tell me if you ever want to pick the spot” signals you’re open but not running the show. Or after you host once, casually add “Next time we can do it at your place or wherever works for you.” People usually take that as a cue to offer without you having to push.\n\nKeeping the tone light and matter‑of‑fact makes it feel natural rather than needy or awkward.\nUser turn 7: Can you give me a few specific scripts I can text people—one for proposing a daytime hang, one for an at-home low-key plan, and one for politely passing on an evening invite while offering an alternative—so it’s clear I’m interested but not always trying to host or coordinate everything?\nAssistant turn 8: Here are a few you can copy‑paste as needed:\n\nFor a daytime hang:  \n“Hey, I’m around this weekend if you feel like a daytime coffee or walk. If you’ve got a spot you like, I’m happy to meet you there.”\n\nFor a low‑key at‑home plan that doesn’t make you the default host:  \n“I’m in the mood for a chill hang sometime—maybe a movie or just working on our own stuff in the same room. If you ever want to host or pick the plan, I’m totally up for that.”\n\nFor declining an evening invite but offering an alternative:  \n“Thanks for the invite! I’m laying low tonight, but I’d love to see you another time. If you’re free this weekend during the day, want to do something then?”\n\n=== Session 39 [Time: 2024-05-12 19:00] ===\nUser turn 1: I’m trying to plan the next couple months in a way that actually keeps my energy up—lately I feel most recharged when I’m out in the middle of things—checking out new spots, meeting people, and keeping my calendar full with plans. Can you help me brainstorm a bunch of low-effort but fun ideas I can rotate through (weeknights + weekends), and maybe a simple system for picking them so I don’t default to staying in?\nAssistant turn 2: You can think of this as building a “menu” of easy go‑to plans so your social energy stays high without needing to think too hard.\n\nFor weeknights, aim for things you can say yes to on short notice. That could be dropping by a café or bar you haven’t tried yet, walking a new neighborhood with a drink in hand, popping into a bookstore or small event, or inviting one friend to join you for something simple like a quick dinner, trivia, or a low‑key open mic. These take almost no prep, get you out of the house, and still feel like real plans.\n\nWeekends can stay just as effortless if you frame them as mini outings rather than big productions. Think checking out a new market, spending an hour at a museum or gallery, heading to a park with a coffee, trying a new brunch spot, or saying yes to one small community event. The key is choosing things that feel like “out in the world” without requiring commitment or planning.\n\nTo keep yourself from defaulting to staying in, treat your ideas like a deck of cards. Make a short list of options in your notes app, shuffle through it when you’re deciding, and pick the first one that doesn’t feel like a chore. You can also set theme nights, like “new place Tuesday” or “wander somewhere Saturday,” so you always know the general shape of your plan without having to choose from scratch. And if making micro‑commitments helps, you can set a rule like “leave the house for at least 30 minutes,” because once you’re out, momentum usually takes care of the rest.\n\nIf you want, I can help you build your personal “deck” based on your vibe, city, or interests.\nUser turn 3: Yeah—can we make that “deck of cards” list more concrete? I’d love like 30–40 ideas split into weeknights vs weekends, and maybe categories (social, solo-but-around-people, active, food/drink, culture). Also, I’m more likely to go if it’s within ~20 minutes and doesn’t require tickets in advance—can you bias the ideas toward that?\nAssistant turn 4: Sure. I’ll give you the 30–40 ideas, but instead of bullets I’ll group them into short, fluid lists inside each category so it still feels conversational.\n\nFor weeknights, good social options could be things like dropping into a neighborhood bar for a quick drink, meeting one friend for a short dinner at a spot you’ve been curious about, swinging by a casual meetup that doesn’t require RSVPs, or joining a pickup board‑game table at a café that has them. Solo-but-around-people ideas might be heading to a cozy café to read or work on something light, sitting at a bar with a snack and people-watching, wandering through a grocery store you’ve never been to, or doing a quick lap of a bookstore before closing. Active options could be taking a 20‑minute neighborhood walk with music, checking out a nearby park just to loop it once, or doing a short bike ride to grab a drink somewhere. Food and drink things could be trying a new happy-hour spot, grabbing dessert at a place you’ve never tried, or doing a “mini food crawl” of two places within the same block. Cultural ideas could be stopping into a gallery that keeps evening hours, catching part of an open mic or small performance you can walk into, or visiting a public library branch you haven’t checked out.\n\nFor weekends, you can go a bit bigger while still keeping it easy. Social options could be inviting a friend to wander a farmers market, meeting someone for an outdoor coffee walk, joining a drop‑in class that doesn’t require signups, or hopping between a few bars in a new-to-you area. Solo-but-around-people ideas might be sitting in a busy park with a good drink, exploring a new neighborhood on foot, checking out a hotel lobby or rooftop bar just to hang for a bit, or browsing a vintage shop. Active options could be doing a short urban hike, biking to a nearby waterfront or lookout, or renting a scooter and cruising around for half an hour. Food and drink things could be trying a new brunch spot, grabbing a pastry and taking it on a walk, exploring a cluster of street-food stalls, or doing a quick “one dish” visit to someplace you’ve always meant to try. Cultural options might include hitting a small museum or exhibit, walking through a weekend market or craft fair, popping into an artist studio open day, or going to a casual afternoon performance you can enter on the spot.\n\nTo make it easier to use, treat each category as a suit in your deck. On weeknights, pick one suit and choose the idea that feels easiest. On weekends, pick two suits and combine them—like a brunch spot followed by a walk, or a market visit followed by a bar. If you'd like, tell me your city or your preferred vibe and I can tailor the whole deck to your area.\nUser turn 5: This is a good start, but can you make it an actual numbered list with like 40 specific “cards” (so I can copy/paste into Notes), clearly labeled Weeknights vs Weekends and tagged with categories? Also can you add a super simple pick-rule—like a 60-second decision flow—for when I’m tired after work and tempted to stay in?\nAssistant turn 6: Here’s a clean, copy‑paste‑friendly list of 40 “cards.” You asked for a numbered list, so I’ll use one even though I normally avoid